/*--------------------------------*- Choupo -*--------------------------------*\
  species/HMal.dat -- CASE-LOCAL model species for the wine tutorial ed08.

  No catalogue record of this species exists (data/standards/species/ carries
  the tartrate family and not the malate family): identity AND transport are
  declared here.  No aqueousThermo block -- no formation datum was read and
  none is invented; the stack prices no enthalpy of this ion.
  THE TRANSPORT BLOCK IS AN ESTIMATE AND SAYS SO.  No limiting ionic
  conductivity of this anion could be READ from a primary source in the
  session that wrote this record (the measurements exist and are named in
  `notes` below, behind paywalls); a value written from memory would be an
  invisible falsehood, so the D0 is derived by a stated method from facts the
  tree already carries, marked `origin estimated; reviewStatus unverified;`
  and ANNOUNCED by the engine on every run that reads it.  Promotion to
  data/standards/ is a curation act reserved to a human review against the
  named primaries.
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
recordType modelSpecies;
schemaVersion 1;

name    HMal;
formula     "C4H5O5-";
charge  -1;
MW { value 133.08; unit g/mol;}

transport
{
    D0 { value 7.860e-10; unit m2/s; }
    provenance
    {
        source  "no primary read; derived from the tree's own records as stated in `method`";
        licence "this record's derivation is the project's own; the inputs are the catalogue's, cited in their own records";
        D0
        {
            origin        estimated;
            method        "the bitartrate estimate (species/HTart.dat, D0 7.568e-10 m2/s from a crystal-volume Stokes radius of 3.243e-10 m) scaled by the cube root of the molar-mass ratio (133.08/149.08)^(1/3) = 0.9629 as the radius ratio -- malate is tartrate minus one hydroxyl -- so D0 = 7.568e-10/0.9629";
            reviewStatus  unverified;
            notes         "Verify against a conductance study of malic acid or its alkali salts (none could be read here; the Bester-Rogac/Apelblat series on tartrates is the template such a measurement would follow) or against Robinson and Stokes, Electrolyte Solutions, 2nd ed. (1959) Appendix 6.1 if it lists the anion.";
        }
    }
}
